  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 213 PM CDT Wed Sep 30 2026\n\nAs a clipper system in Canada passes to the north, very light\nshowers are moving through areas along the ND/ Canada\nInternational border with breezy northwest winds across the \nstate. Cool temperatures will follow suit of this mostly dry \ncold front as surface high pressure moves the system eastward. \nOvernight tonight, low temperatures will dip into the mid 30s to\nmid 40s with high temperature tomorrow in the mid 50s to mid \n60s. With these cool overnight lows, there may be some localized\nareas with frost in the northern portion of the state. As \nsurface high pressure settles into the area tomorrow, cool air \nwill remain with less windy conditions. Because of this, \novernight temperatures Thursday into Friday will dip lower than \ntonight with lows in the 30s to low 40s and more widespread \nfrost potential for northern areas. While this would be the \nfirst widespread frost of the this fall season, it is \nclimatologically normal. \n \nBy Friday, the surface high pressure will move out of the region\nas a developing surface front to our west will bring breezy \nsoutherly flow. As a result an elevated warm front looks to \nmove through, followed by a weak clipper system Friday night. \nLow to medium chances for showers could be found from either of \nthese features. Temperatures could warm slightly, yet overall \nremain in the 60s for highs. Cloudy conditions could also bring \nslightly warmer temperatures Friday night into Saturday morning,\nwith lows forecast in the mid 30s to mid 40s.\n\nSaturday morning some lingering showers could be present as a\nresult of the aforementioned clipper system with near seasonable\nhigh temperatures in the 60s and breezy northwest winds. Dry high\npressure conditions return overnight Saturday with low \ntemperature returning to the 30s. Zonal flow will set up early\nnext week that could bring weak disturbances and low chances for\nprecipitation. This westerly flow could also bring some warming\ntemperatures, with forecast highs currently in the mid 60s to\nmid 70s Monday and Tuesday. Milder morning lows would also be\nfound with this pattern. An increased gradient at the surface\ncould also bring some breezy southerly winds on Monday.",
